where Ā_k means remaining masked candidate positions.

Greedy anchor selection
The greedy procedure is:

A_0 = ∅

For k rounds:
    try every remaining masked position p
    reveal ground-truth token x_p
    run one forward pass
    compute total IG from p
    choose best p
    add p to anchor set
So round 1 answers:

Is there any single anchor token whose reveal helps many other positions?

Later rounds answer:

Does the model naturally choose spread-out anchors, or does it keep choosing prefix-like/local anchors?

The document calls k=1 the “existence probe,” because it only checks whether useful nonlocal anchors exist at all. 


Why layout controls matter
Greedy search can be noisy. It may pick weird positions due to token frequency, punctuation, or idiosyncratic model confidence.

So the document proposes hand-specified layouts:

Layout	Meaning
Prefix	first k masked positions
Spread	anchors maximally separated
Cluster-mid	k consecutive middle positions
This is the key causal control.

At matched k, all layouts reveal the same number of tokens. So if spread beats prefix, the effect is about spatial placement, not simply more information.

Where this should work best
The document correctly predicts that the effect will be domain-dependent. 


Strongest domains:

1. Code skeletons
Example:

def foo(x):
    [MASK]
    return y
If the model sees both function signature and return structure, it can infer the middle better. Code has delimiters, indentation, repeated variables, and long-range constraints.

2. Templates / slot filling
Example:

Name: [MASK]
Date: [MASK]
Total: [MASK]
Payment method: [MASK]
Distant fields are semantically coupled. Seeing one slot can constrain another.

3. Parallel lists
Example:

France - Paris
Germany - Berlin
Italy - [MASK]
Spain - [MASK]
Anchors far apart reveal the repeated schema.

Weakest domain:

Free-form continuation
Plain prose may still be dominated by local L2R priors. Spread anchors may help less unless there is strong discourse structure.

What result would be interesting
A strong positive result would look like this:

spread score > prefix score
spread IG tail has nonlocal mass
greedy-selected anchors are far apart
In words:

Revealing a distant anchor improves confidence not just nearby, but across the sequence.

A weak/null result:

prefix ≈ spread
influence mostly local
greedy picks near-prefix tokens
That would mean MDM confidence decoding is still mostly behaving like L2R AR decoding.

The most important plot to make
For each candidate anchor p, plot influence over target positions q:

x-axis: q position
y-axis: I_{p,q} or IG_{p,q}
Then compare:

prefix anchor
middle anchor
suffix anchor
spread anchor set
The signature you want is a nonlocal influence tail: anchor at position 90 improves confidence at positions 30–70, not only 89–91.

