The model is often overconfident at the far left and far right masked positions. It also gives generic tokens like space/newline high probability at many positions, so confidence is not position-specific. The paper observes this on LLaDA-8B and DREAM-7B. 


Existing sampler: sort-and-select
One decoding step:

1. Run model on masked sequence.
2. For every masked position, get token distribution.
3. Sort positions by confidence.
4. Select several positions whose entropy/error budget is acceptable.
5. Fill those positions.
This is faster than one-token-at-a-time decoding.

Problem: confidence is biased, so sorting by confidence is bad.

Their fix: PCG
Position Contrastive Guidance = make confidence position-specific.

Two parts.

1. SLR: soft left-to-right bias
They multiply confidence by a decaying function:

score(i) = σ(i) * max_v p(v | z, i)
So early masked positions are favored, far-right positions are suppressed.

Why? Instruct MDMs are too confident about far-right EOS/PAD/space-like tokens.

2. Contrastive position guidance
They penalize tokens that are likely everywhere.

Formula:

p_PCG(v | z, i) ∝ p(v | z, i)^(1+λ) / p_ref(v | z)^λ
Meaning:

good token = high probability at this position
bad token = high probability at many positions
Example:

space token: high everywhere → penalize
number "6": high only here → boosted
So PCG prefers informative, position-specific tokens.

