Minimal explanation: UNCODE paper
This paper studies why masked diffusion models decode badly when they use naive confidence/entropy-based unmasking.

Main claim:

Uncertainty-based decoding has two biases:
1. Rigid boundary bias
2. Trivial token bias
Rigid boundary bias means the model unmasks the left and right ends first, then moves inward. This creates a U-shaped decoding trajectory. For reasoning tasks, this is bad because the model may reveal the final answer before building the reasoning chain.

Trivial token bias means the model prefers easy tokens like spaces, punctuation, newlines, “the”, “of”, etc. These tokens are high-confidence but low-information. Revealing them early wastes decoding steps and can lock the model into bad formatting.

Their method is UNCODE.

It changes the unmasking score:

calibrated score = positional prior × semantic prior × raw uncertainty score
The positional prior controls decoding order. It can make decoding more left-to-right when the task needs sequential reasoning.

P_i = exp(-λ i)
Larger λ = more left-to-right.

Smaller λ = more flexible / global decoding.

The semantic prior penalizes frequent boring tokens:

S_i = -log p_corpus(predicted_token)
So rare/contentful tokens get higher priority, while spaces and punctuation get lower priority.

Main result:

UNCODE improves MDM decoding by fixing bad unmasking order.
They test on:

HumanEval, MBPP, GSM8K, MATH500, GPQA, Countdown, Sudoku
and models:

LLaDA-8B-Instruct
LLaDA-1.5-8B
Dream-v0-Instruct-7B
They report over 7% average improvement over decoding baselines, and show it can combine with faster samplers like EB-Sampler and Fast-dLLM.

